From 71216b399febfffae383ba1b4f784c4fd4f90843 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:33:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LinuxSyscalls: emulate syscall user dispatch
 (PR_SET_SYSCALL_USER_DISPATCH) as Linux 6.11 does

Wine's x86-64 ntdll enables it per thread so a Windows program's own
`syscall` reaches its dispatcher as a SIGSYS. FEX passed the prctl to the
host, which can't do it for guest code. Now the prctl keeps per-thread
state (off in new threads and fork children) and every guest syscall is
checked first, before ptrace and seccomp: outside the range and not
allowed by the selector, it is delivered as SIGSYS (si_code
SYS_USER_DISPATCH) through the SECCOMP_RET_TRAP frame path and doesn't
run. A bad selector value, an unreadable selector or a blocked/ignored
SIGSYS kill the process as Linux does (ForceExitSignal, generalised from
ForceGuestSigSegv).

The trap path's post-syscall RIP is now taken only by the SIGSYS queued
for it: another signal arriving while QueueSignal makes its syscalls took
it before, and the SIGSYS was then never sent (a Windows syscall
returned its own number).
---
